---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="VAST Networks">
<main class="case">
  <div class="case-hero shell">
    <a class="case-back" href="/work">← All work</a>
    <div class="eyebrow">Selected Work · 2016–2018</div>
    <h1>VAST Networks</h1>
    <p class="case-lead">Operational leadership during the rapid deployment of a large scale WiFi network, with responsibility for engineering, support, monitoring, vendors and operational performance.</p>
    <div class="case-meta"><span>Period</span><strong>2016–2018</strong><span>Role</span><strong>Head of Operations</strong></div>
  </div>

  <section class="shell case-grid">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Scale</div>
      <h2>Taking network operations into a different kind of scale.</h2>
      <p>At VAST Networks, the focus shifted from executive technology strategy into the operational leadership required to deploy and run a very large WiFi network.</p>
      <p>The network deployment covered more than 2,000 locations and more than 25,000 access points, requiring operational discipline as well as technical capability.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="case-panel">
      <div class="eyebrow">At a glance</div>
      <div class="stat"><strong>2,000+</strong><span>locations</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><strong>25,000+</strong><span>access points</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><strong>20+</strong><span>engineering and support team</span></div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-grid reverse">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Operations</div>
      <h2>Building the capability to operate at scale.</h2>
      <p>The role included leadership of a more than 20 person engineering and support team, with responsibility for improving network reliability and performance.</p>
      <p>Operational monitoring was improved to support rapid scaling while maintaining cost control. This is a different form of technology leadership from designing a network. The challenge becomes making the operating model reliable enough to sustain growth.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="case-panel">
      <div class="eyebrow">Operating priorities</div>
      <div class="step"><b>01</b> Reliability</div>
      <div class="step"><b>02</b> Monitoring</div>
      <div class="step"><b>03</b> Scalability</div>
      <div class="step"><b>04</b> Cost control</div>
      <div class="step"><b>05</b> Team leadership</div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-grid">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Partners and suppliers</div>
      <h2>Technology operations are also commercial operations.</h2>
      <p>The role included vendor negotiations and the development of strategic partnerships, with the objective of securing the capability needed for deployment while reducing unnecessary expense.</p>
      <p>That combination of technical operations, people leadership and commercial negotiation is consistent with the broader career, where technology decisions have repeatedly been treated as business decisions rather than as isolated IT concerns.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Network Operations</span><span>WiFi</span><span>Team Leadership</span><span>Vendor Management</span><span>Scalability</span></div>
    </article>
    <aside class="case-note"><strong>A useful career bridge</strong><p>VAST sits between the earlier telecom and Internet infrastructure career and the later digital health programme leadership work. It brought the focus back to practical delivery at substantial operational scale.</p></aside>
  </section>

  <section class="case-dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="eyebrow">The VAST period</div>
      <h2>Large scale infrastructure, people and operational delivery.</h2>
      <p>The period demonstrates a recurring theme in the career, moving between strategy and hands on delivery while keeping technology connected to organisational and commercial outcomes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="shell case-next"><div class="eyebrow">Next</div><a href="/work/verizon-mtn">Verizon Business to MTN Business →</a></div>
</main>
</BaseLayout>
